## cimt-claude-talend integration

This project uses the [cimt-claude-talend](https://github.com/mkcimt/cimt-claude-talend) toolkit.

**Find the kit's location, in this exact order:**

1. **Read `.claude/cimt-claude-talend.path`** in the project root. The file contains one line — the absolute path of the kit's checkout. **This is the primary mechanism.** It's written by `setup/install.py` and survives any shell-rc / env-var weirdness (especially on Windows where Claude Code may not see PowerShell-profile env vars).
2. If the file is missing or empty, **fall back** to the environment variable `CIMT_TALEND_PATTERNS`.
3. If both are missing: **STOP. Do not search the filesystem.** Tell the user: *"The cimt-claude-talend kit is not installed or not visible in this session. Please run `setup/bootstrap.sh` (macOS/Linux) or `setup/bootstrap.ps1` (Windows) from the kit's checkout, then start a new Claude Code session."* Wait for the user — do not proceed with Talend-mechanical work without the kit.

Once you know the kit's path (call it `$KIT`), **consult `$KIT/knowledge/` first** for anything Talend-mechanical — see `$KIT/knowledge/INDEX.md` for the index. Topics covered:

- Reading `.item` files, Studio quirks, citation conventions — `knowledge/mechanics/`
- TMC API, deploy / promote / bind, microservice lifecycle, known bugs — `knowledge/tmc/`
- Maven build, Cloud Publisher, headless release runbook — `knowledge/build-publish/`
- Optional Talend patterns (context variables, batch frameworks, …) — `knowledge/patterns/`. Each pattern file explains how to **detect from the project's artifacts** which variant is in use — derive from the code, don't expect a pre-declaration.
- Code review principles and Talend heuristics — `knowledge/code-review/`
- Interface documentation conventions — `knowledge/documentation/`

Prefer these files over guessing from training data. They are empirically maintained.

## Editing protocol — `.item` files

`.item` files are deceptively edit-friendly XML with several Studio-only invariants. Before touching one, **read `$KIT/knowledge/mechanics/item-editing-programmatic.md` once** and run through its pre-edit checklist for every write.

**Hard rules — non-negotiable:**

1. **Line endings stay CRLF on Windows.** Talend Studio writes `.item` with CRLF; the project convention is CRLF; mixing LF into one file while siblings are CRLF interacts badly with code-gen. Verify with `file <path>` after every write. Programmatic writes should use binary I/O (`read_bytes` / `write_bytes`) or explicit `newline=""` — never bare `Path.write_text` on Windows. Full rationale and verification commands in `item-editing-programmatic.md`.
2. **Sibling `.properties` touch.** Update `modificationDate` and `productVersion` in the same turn and commit both files together. Use the helper: `python $KIT/tools/touch_item_properties.py <path>`. Studio does this automatically on its own saves; code-only edits do it explicitly. Detail in `$KIT/knowledge/mechanics/item-properties-touch.md`.
3. **`<elementParameter>`-internal id uniqueness.** When cloning a block that contains `id="N"` attributes (TRACE_COLUMN lists, MAPPING tables, SCHEMA_COLUMN lists, etc.), bump every id by a high constant so the clone doesn't collide with the original. Verify with the `Counter`-based snippet in `item-editing-programmatic.md`.
4. **Embedded entity preservation.** `&#xD;&#xA;`, `&#13;&#10;`, `&#x9;` etc. inside MEMO_JAVA / MEMO_SQL values must survive byte-for-byte. Never decode them to literal newlines / tabs.
5. **Post-edit verification.** Re-check CRLF, re-check no duplicate ids, re-check XML parses, re-check `git diff` shows only the intended change (not a whole-file diff caused by line-ending flip).

**Before doing heavy schema additions:** if the affected joblet is inlined many times by a route (`grep -c 'componentName="<joblet>"' <route>.item`), measure the multiplier — see `$KIT/knowledge/mechanics/joblet-inlining.md`. Adding one column to a heavily inlined joblet's schema can add hundreds of KB to the generated `.java`. The Eclipse JDT code formatter starts OOMing on generated files past ~30 MB; the route may compile fine before your change and fail after.

**When code-gen fails after a `.item` edit:** the visible NPE `Cannot invoke "String.getBytes()" because "processCode" is null` is almost always a downstream symptom. Open `<workspace>/.metadata/.log`, find the error preceding the NPE — usually an `OutOfMemoryError` in `DefaultCodeFormatter`. Diagnosis flow in `$KIT/knowledge/mechanics/studio-clean-and-codegen.md` (which also documents how to force a regenerate — Studio has no Project → Clean menu, you do it by hand).

## Configuration discovery

Two config files in this project's `.claude/`:

- `talend.properties` — project-shared (committed). Holds `talend.project.name`, `talend.p2.update.url`, TMC region/workspace/URLs, env chain.
- `talend.local.properties` — per-developer (gitignored). Holds `talend.studio.path`, `talend.framework.path`, `tmc.pat`.

**Before running any operation that needs a value from these files, check the relevant config.** The user is not expected to fill these in by hand. Use this order:

1. **Try to read** the value from the relevant file via `$KIT/tools/cli.py get <project> <key>`.
2. **If empty, try to derive** from project artifacts:
   - `talend.project.name` — folder containing `talend.project`
   - `studio.version` — from `<project-root>/<talend.project.name>/talend.project` (`productVersion` attribute)
   - `talend.p2.update.url` — from `studio.version` (R-tag → `https://update.talend.com/Studio/8/updates/R<YYYY-MM>/`)
   - `tmc.publish.url` — from `tmc.region` (`https://tmc.<region>.cloud.talend.com/inventory/`)
3. **If still missing, ask the user** with context: explain *why* the value is needed and what good values look like. Don't just ask "what's your `tmc.workspace`?" — say "I need the TMC workspace name to publish. It's the same name across all envs (dev/tst/uat/prd) and is visible in your Talend Cloud UI URL. What is it?"
4. **Persist the answer** via `$KIT/tools/cli.py set <project> <key> <value>`. The CLI routes keys to the correct file (local vs shared) automatically.
5. **For the TMC PAT** (`tmc.pat`): never derive, never log, never echo back. If absent, **ask the user to paste their PAT into the chat**, then immediately store it via `python $KIT/tools/cli.py set <project> tmc.pat <pasted-value>`. Do *not* suggest the user run `setup/store_pat.py` — that's a terminal-only helper for users who prefer not to paste into chat, but the in-chat path is faster and the default. The PAT is a secret: do not echo it back, do not log it. After storing, confirm with "PAT stored." and nothing more.
6. **For `talend.studio.path` and `talend.framework.path`**: never guess or use defaults silently. Always confirm with the user before persisting — wrong paths are confusing to debug later.

## Operational vs. documentation reads

Build, publish, bind, promote, deploy, undeploy, redeploy, status, review — **always read live state** from the project files and TMC. Never trust cached summaries, previous-session outputs, or values that may be stale. Documentation commands may consult inventories but must verify live behaviour where it matters. Full principle: `$KIT/knowledge/mechanics/operational-vs-documentation.md`.

## Scratch files — `.claude/tmp/`

Any temporary script, JSON dump, report, or one-off helper you generate during a session **must** be written to `.claude/tmp/` in the project root (create the directory if it doesn't exist). The folder is gitignored, so Studio's Git Staging view won't surface noise from Claude's working files.

**Never** drop scratch files in the project root, in `.claude/` directly, or anywhere under `TALEND_DARE/` — those locations end up either tracked by git or flagged as untracked changes by Studio. See `$KIT/knowledge/mechanics/scratch-files.md` for the full rule.

## Doctor on first use

The first time you load a knowledge file or invoke a skill in this session, run `$KIT/setup/doctor.py <project>` once. If it reports problems, surface them to the user with the fix hints and pause. If it reports OK, proceed silently — don't repeat doctor again in the same session.

## Updates

When the user asks to *"update cimt-claude-talend"* (or equivalent), run `python $KIT/setup/update.py <project>` — passing the current project path. The script pulls the kit's repo, reports what changed, then automatically re-runs install.py on the project so the CLAUDE.md integration block, .gitignore, and symlinks are refreshed in one go. If skills or agents changed, the script tells the user to restart their Claude Code session; surface that hint.

## Capture Discipline

When a non-trivial insight surfaces during work:

1. Classify the layer:
   - **2a** — universal Talend truth (mechanics, TMC API behaviour, Studio quirks)
   - **2b** — optional pattern (a framework, a component a project may or may not use)
   - **3** — project-specific (this project's conventions, business glossary, KEDB)
   - **4** — developer-specific (paths, personal preferences, machine setup)
2. Route accordingly:
   - 2a / 2b → cimt-claude-talend repo (`$KIT`), file under `knowledge/...`
   - 3 → this project, under `docs/`
   - 4 → `.claude/talend.local.properties` (paths) or user memory (preferences)
3. **Memory is for Layer 4 only.** Anything that should survive `rm -rf ~/.claude` belongs in a repo.

If classification is unclear, ask once. Default to Layer 3 unless you can articulate the mechanic.

See `$KIT/CONTRIBUTING.md` for the full capture flow.

